set \\(\\{L_j\\mid j\\notin I\\}\\) contains \\(n-|I|\\) distinct vertical lines.  \nIf \\(i,i'\\in I\\) with \\(i\\neq i'\\), the slopes of \\(S_{i,b_i}\\) and \\(S_{i',b_{i'}}\\) are \\(\\frac{b_i-1}{i-1}\\) and \\(\\frac{b_{i'}-1}{i'-1}\\) respectively; because the denominators \\(i-1\\) and \\(i'-1\\) differ, the slopes differ, so the two lines are distinct.  \nA sunny line cannot be vertical, therefore no \\(S_{i,b_i}\\) coincides with any \\(L_j\\).  \nHence \\(\\mathcal F_I\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n(b) Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n\n*If \\(a\\notin I\\)*, then the vertical line \\(L_a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_I\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n\n*If \\(a\\in I\\)*, then by definition \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\); we may choose the parameter \\(b_a\\) equal to this \\(b\\).  \nConsequently the line \\(S_{a,b_a}=S_{a,b}\\) (which is part of \\(\\mathcal F_I\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n(c) By Lemma\u202f2 each line \\(S_{i,b_i}\\) is sunny, while each vertical line \\(L_j\\) is not sunny.  Therefore the number of sunny lines equals the cardinality of \\(I\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Upper bound on the number of sunny lines)\n\nIn any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, at most \\(n-1\\) of them can be sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the column  \n\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subseteq T_n .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(C_1\\) have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate.  \nA non\u2011vertical line meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one point, so a single sunny (i.e. non\u2011vertical) line can contain at most one element of \\(C_1\\).  \nSince \\(|C_1|=n\\), covering all points of \\(C_1\\) requires at least one vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is not sunny.  \nHence any admissible family must contain at least one non\u2011sunny line, i.e. the number of sunny lines is \\(\\le n-1\\). \