roved that for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nNo larger value of \\(k\\) is possible because the point \\((1,1)\\) forces the line \\(x=1\\) (which is not sunny) to be present.  \nHence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its direction vector is not parallel to \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\); equivalently its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Vertical baseline (all lines non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k=0\\)).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(L_i:\\;x=i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,n)\\) are distinct, none is sunny, and they cover every point of \\(T_n\\) because each point has its first coordinate \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).\n\n3. **A sunny line that covers a prescribed point of a column.**  \n   For any column index \\(i\\ge 2\\) and any integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{i,b}:\\; y=\\frac{b-1}{\\,i-1\\,}(x-1)+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   * The slope \\(\\dfrac{b-1}{i-1}\\) is finite, non‑zero and different from \\(-1\\); therefore \\(S_{i,b}\\) is sunny.  \n   * Substituting \\(x=i\\) gives \\(y=b\\); hence \\((i,b)\\in S_{i,b}\\).  \n   * Because its slope is finite, \\(S_{i,b}\\) is not vertical, so it cannot coincide with any line \\(x=j\\).\n\n4. **Replacing vertical lines by sunny lines.**  \n   Choose any subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\in I\\) pick an arbitrary admissible \\(b_i\\) (i.e. \\(1\\le b_i\\le n+1-i\\)) and form the line \\(S_{i,b_i}\\).  \n   Keep the vertical lines \\(L_j\\) for all \\(j\\notin I\\) (including necessarily \\(L_1\\)).  \n   The resulting family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_I=\\bigl\\{S_{i,b_i}\\mid i\\in I\\bigr\\}\\cup\n               \\bigl\\{L_j\\mid j\\notin I\\bigr\\}\n   \\]\n\n   has exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, contains \\(|I|\\) sunny lines, and covers every point of \\(T_n\\):\n   *If a point lies in column \\(j\\notin I\\) it is on the vertical line \\(L_j\\);  \n   if it lies in column \\(i\\in I\\) it is on the sunny line \\(S_{i,b_i}\\) because we may choose \\(b_i\\) equal to the point’s second coordinate.*\n\n5. **Achievable values of \\(k\\).**  \n   Since \\(I\\) can be any subset of \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\), \\(|I|\\) can be any integer from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-1\\).  \n   Thus for each \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   All points with first coordinate \\(1\\) belong to the set  \n\n   \\[\n   C_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subseteq T_n .\n   \\]\n\n   Any non‑vertical line meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one point, so a single sunny line can contain at most one element of \\(C_1\\).  \n   Because there are \\(n\\) distinct points in \\(C_1\\), covering all of them requires the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is not sunny.  \n   Consequently any admissible family must contain at least one non‑sunny line, i.e. \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\nCombining points 4–6 we obtain the complete description of the possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Vertical baseline)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and they cover all