e following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that **every** integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\ncan occur as the number of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfies the two requirements of the problem.  \nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds by **induction on \\(n\\)** and uses only elementary counting together with an explicit geometric construction.  \n\n* **Base step \\(n=3\\).**  \n  All six points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le4\\) are  \n\n  \\[\n  (1,1),(1,2),(2,1).\n  \\]\n\n  With three lines we can realise every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) by the obvious\n  choices (all vertical, one vertical + two sunny, two vertical + one sunny,\n  three sunny).  Hence the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n* **Inductive step.**  \n  Assume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge 3\\); i.e. for every\n  \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) of\n  \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points  \n\n  \\[\n  S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n  \\]\n\n  and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n  From \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) we construct families for \\(n+1\\).\n\n  1. **Keeping the same number of sunny lines.**  \n     Add the vertical line  \n\n     \\[\n     x=n+1\n     \\]\n\n     (which is not sunny).  All new points with abscissa \\(n+1\\) are\n     \\((n+1,1)\\); they are covered by the added line.\n     The points whose coordinates satisfy \\(a+b=n+2\\) are  \n\n     \\[\n     (1,n+1),(2,n),\\dots ,(n+1,1).\n     \\]\n\n     Every one of them already lies on the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\) or on a\n     line of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) (the latter because the set\n     \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+2\\}\\) is obtained from the set\n     \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\) by translating the whole picture one unit to\n     the right).  Consequently the enlarged family  \n\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)=\\mathcal L_n(k)\\cup\\{x=n+1\\}\n     \\]\n\n     consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, still contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny\n     lines, and covers all points of \\(S_{n+1}\\).\n\n  2. **Increasing the number of sunny lines by one.**  \n     Start again from \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) and add the **diagonal line**\n\n     \\[\n     x+y=n+2 .\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n     The line (1) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to\n     \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is sunny.  It contains precisely the new points\n     with sum \\(n+2\\), i.e. the whole set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+2\\}\\).\n     As before we also add the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\) to cover the point\n     \\((n+1,1)\\).  The resulting family  \n\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal L_{n+1}(k+1)=\\mathcal L_n(k)\\cup\\{x=n+1,\\;x+y=n+2\\}\n     \\]\n\n     has \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k+1\\) of which are sunny, and\n     covers all points of \\(S_{n+1}\\).\n\n  By the two constructions we can pass from any admissible \\(k\\) for\n  \\(n\\) to both \\(k\\) and \\(k+1\\) for \\(n+1\\).  Since the base case supplies\n  all values \\(0,1,2,3\\) for \\(n=3\\), an easy induction shows that for every\n  \\(n\\ge3\\) each integer \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is attainable.\n\n* **Conclusion.**  \n  The induction yields a complete description of the possible numbers of\n  sunny lines: **all** integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) inclusive occur, and no\n  other integer can occur because the total number of lines is \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN_n=|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Base case)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) and every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) there exists a family of three\ndistinct lines covering \\(S_3\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nk & \\text{family of three lines} \\\\ \\hline\n0 & \\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\} \\\\\n1 & \\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=2\\} \\\\\n2 & \\{x=1,\\; \\text{line through }(1,2)\\text{ of slope }2,\n        \\text{ line through }(2,1)\\text{ of slope }3\\}\\\\\n3 & \\{\\text{line through }(1,1)\\text{ of slope }2,\n        \\text{ line through }(1,2)\\text{ of slope }3,\n        \\text{ line through }(2,1)\\text{ of slope }5\\}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAll three lines are distinct, the required points belong to at least one of\nthem, and the number of sunny lines is as indicated. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Inductive step)\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) and a given integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying\n\n* every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) are sunny.\n\nThen\n\n* there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)\\) of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines\n  covering \\(S_{n+1}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines;\n* there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k+1)\\) of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines\n  covering \\(S_{n+1}\\) and containing exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nS_{n+1}=S_n\\;\\cup\\;\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+2\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe new points are precisely the lattice points on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{n+1}:x+y=n+2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**(i) Construction of \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)\\).**  \nAdd the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nv_{n+1}:x=n+1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(v_{n+1}\\) is not sunny.  It contains the unique new point\n\\((n+1,1)\\).  Every other new point belongs to \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\); but each such\npoint also belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) because translating the\nwhole picture one unit to the right sends the set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\)\nonto \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+2\\}\\) and the translation preserves collinearity.\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)=\\mathcal L_n(k)\\cup\\{v_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(S_{n+1}\\), consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines,\nand still has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**(ii) Construction of \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k+1)\\).**  \nStart again from \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) and add the two lines  \n\n\\[\nv_{n+1}:x=n+1 ,\\qquad\nd_{n+1}:x+y=n+2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(d_{n+1}\\) has slope \\(-1\\)?  No, its equation is\n\\(x+y=n+2\\), i.e. slope \\(-1\\).  **But** the definition of a sunny line\nexcludes only the line *parallel* to \\(x+y=0\\); a line *coincident* with\n\\(x+y=0\\) is also parallel, hence not sunny.  To obtain a sunny line we\ninstead use the line  \n\n\\[\ns_{n+1}:x+y=n+2+\\varepsilon ,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\varepsilon\\) is any non‑zero real number (for instance \\(\\varepsilon=1\\)).\nThe line \\(s_{n+1}\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) **only** when its slope is\n\\(-1\\); the slope of (6) is still \\(-1\\), but the line is *not* parallel\nto \\(x+y=0\\) because parallelism depends only on the direction, not on the\nintercept.  Hence any line of the form \\(x+y=c\\) is **not** sunny; we must\nchoose a line whose slope is different from \\(-1\\).  Therefore we replace\n(6) by a line of a different slope, for instance\n\n\\[\ns_{n+1}: y = 2x - (n) .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe line (7) is sunny (its slope is \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  Moreover,\nfor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(b=n+2-a\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nb = 2a - n \\iff a